{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4cb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07b9e-4019-7d82-8599-f430d1ae00b2"},"sequence":192,"recorded_at":1780531400000379,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"868a1309-d750-4b15-8a10-0ca2d4130887","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"868a1309-d750-4b15-8a10-0ca2d4130887","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"868a1309-d750-4b15-8a10-0ca2d4130887"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"1ecf48b9-2d7d-4353-b351-6f8d9cad2a4f"},"task_id":"1ecf48b9-2d7d-4353-b351-6f8d9cad2a4f","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"1ecf48b9-2d7d-4353-b351-6f8d9cad2a4f","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.md`.\n(2267 line(s) above)\n2268|### 5.7 Derived session states\n2269|\n2270|The state engine derives exactly one state for each\n2271|<code>(session_id, host_id)</code> projection and separately reports the\n2272|winning owner's projection as the session lifecycle state. Thus a destination\n2273|can be <code>running</code> while an old host is locally <code>stale</code>:\n2274|\n2275|| State | Meaning |\n2276|| --- | --- |\n2277|| <code>creating</code> | Session record and initial lease exist; no first validated checkpoint exists and bootstrap launch/retry is in progress |\n2278|| <code>running</code> | Winning owner has a live provider turn or accepts input |\n2279|| <code>idle</code> | Owner is live at an adapter-proven safe turn boundary |\n2280|| <code>quiescing</code> | New input is blocked while waiting for a safe boundary and clean stop |\n2281|| <code>checkpointing</code> | Safe state is being captured and validated |\n2282|| <code>stopped</code> | Winning owner has no provider process and a non-null validated checkpoint from which it is resumable |\n2283|| <code>materializing</code> | Destination staging/validation is in progress; it is not owner-active |\n2284|| <code>parked</code> | Wrapper exists but refuses provider launch due to remote/unknown ownership |\n2285|| <code>failed</code> | An operation failed and recovery metadata exists |\n2286|| <code>stale</code> | Local process/replica has a losing fencing token |\n2287|| <code>tombstoned</code> | Session is logically deleted and cannot resume |\n2288|\n2289|The exact <code>SessionState</code> enum is, in this order,\n2290|<code>creating</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>idle</code>,\n2291|<code>quiescing</code>, <code>checkpointing</code>, <code>stopped</code>,\n2292|<code>materializing</code>, <code>parked</code>, <code>failed</code>,\n2293|<code>stale</code>, and <code>tombstoned</code>. Every RPC and CLI field typed\n2294|<code>SessionState</code> uses exactly this registry. The spellings\n2295|<code>created</code>, <code>starting</code>, and <code>quiesced</code> are not\n2296|session lifecycle states in Session Record 1.0.0; a subsystem-specific bridge state\n2297|MAY use <code>quiesced</code> only where Section 9 declares that separate enum.\n2298|\n2299|Allowed transitions are:\n2300|\n2301|~~~text\n2302|creating -> running | idle | failed\n2303|running -> idle | quiescing | failed | stale\n2304|idle -> running | quiescing | checkpointing | stopped | stale\n2305|quiescing -> idle | checkpointing | failed | stale\n2306|checkpointing -> idle | stopped | materializing | failed\n2307|materializing -> stopped | running | parked | failed\n2308|stopped -> materializing | running | parked | tombstoned\n2309|parked -> materializing | running | stopped | stale\n2310|failed -> creating | stopped | parked | materializing | stale | tombstoned\n2311|stale -> stopped | tombstoned\n2312|~~~\n2313|\n2314|Any transition not listed MUST fail with <code>invalid_state_transition</code>.\n2315|Force takeover changes the old host's derived state to <code>stale</code> after\n2316|lease convergence even if its process remains alive.\n2317|\n2318|The <code>failed -> creating</code> edge is permitted only for an epoch-1\n2319|session with no validated checkpoint, the same winning create lease, no\n2320|authoritative <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> event, and the Section 13.1\n2321|bootstrap-retry preconditions. A force-closed bootstrap remains\n2322|<code>failed</code> and non-resumable. No event or RPC result may derive\n2323|<code>stopped</code> while <code>newest_checkpoint_id</code> is null.\n2324|\n2325|## 6. Configuration contract\n2326|\n2327|### 6.1 Loading and precedence\n2328|\n2329|Configuration schema <code>urn:ax:schema:config</code> version\n2330|<code>1.0.0</code> is encoded as TOML. Precedence, highest first, is:\n2331|\n2332|1. command flags;\n2333|2. the five documented Section 3.2 <code>AX_*</code> path overrides;\n2334|3. values in the selected configuration file; and\n2335|4. normative defaults in this section.\n2336|\n2337|The configuration file itself is selected by <code>--config</code>, then\n2338|<code>AX_CONFIG</code>, then the platform-default configuration directory's\n2339|<code>config.toml</code>. The data, state, cache, and runtime overrides select\n2340|infrastructure roots and are not TOML field-by-field overrides. The same\n2341|resolved roots MUST be used by the CLI, service, RPC server, provider host, and\n2342|crash recovery for the entire process lifetime.\n2343|\n2344|Unknown top-level keys MUST fail configuration loading. Secret values MUST NOT\n2345|be accepted in config fields; a provider MAY name a machine-local environment\n2346|variable or credential profile.\n2347|\n2348|The complete 1.0.0 environment-override registry is\n2349|<code>AX_CONFIG</code>, <code>AX_DATA_DIR</code>,\n2350|<code>AX_STATE_DIR</code>, <code>AX_CACHE_DIR</code>, and\n2351|<code>AX_RUNTIME_DIR</code>, with the value kinds and empty-value rule in\n2352|Section 3.2. There are no other field-by-field <code>AX_*</code> overrides in\n2353|this version. Provider credential variables are destination-local inputs and\n2354|never configuration values.\n2355|\n2356|### 6.2 Normative example\n2357|\n2358|~~~toml\n2359|schema = \"urn:ax:schema:config\"\n2360|schema_version = \"1.0.0\"\n2361|host_id = \"0198f4c8-4a10-7b22-8b3c-1234567890ab\"\n2362|host_name = \"mbp-ivan\"\n2363|platform = \"macos\"\n2364|\n2365|[mesh]\n2366|transport = \"ssh\"\n2367|sync_interval_seconds = 60\n2368|connect_timeout_seconds = 10\n2369|rpc_timeout_seconds = 300\n2370|workspace_replication = true\n2371|payload_encryption = \"none\"\n2372|\n2373|[[mesh.peers]]\n2374|host_id = \"0198f4c8-7d40-7e55-8e6f-1234567890ab\"\n2375|name = \"workstation\"\n2376|endpoint = \"ivan@workstation.tailnet.ts.net\"\n2377|platform = \"linux\"\n2378|ssh_args = [\"-o\", \"BatchMode=yes\"]\n2379|\n2380|[[mesh.peers.workspace_roots]]\n2381|logical_root = \"relux\"\n2382|path = \"/srv/relux\"\n2383|\n2384|[[workspace_roots]]\n2385|logical_root = \"relux\"\n2386|path = \"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks\"\n2387|\n2388|[providers]\n2389|plugin_dirs = [\"/Users/iv/.local/libexec/ax/providers\"]\n2390|allow_path_plugins = true\n2391|require_explicit_trust = true\n2392|\n2393|[sync]\n2394|chunk_bytes = 4194304\n2395|max_parallel_chunks = 4\n2396|staging_retention_hours = 72\n2397|tombstone_min_retention_days = 90\n2398|\n2399|[terminal]\n2400|backend = \"tmux\"\n2401|safe_boundary_timeout_seconds = 300\n2402|graceful_stop_timeout_seconds = 60\n2403|\n2404|[service]\n2405|enabled = true\n2406|health_interval_seconds = 30\n2407|\n2408|[restore]\n2409|auto_resume = false\n2410|\n2411|[profiles.yolo]\n2412|require_first_use_confirmation = true\n2413|~~~\n2414|\n2415|On native Windows, a workspace mapping is written with a quoted TOML string\n2416|such as <code>path = \"D:\\\\Developer\\\\ReluxWorks\"</code>, and terminal backend\n2417|MUST be <code>conpty</code>. WSL2 configuration uses Linux paths and\n2418|<code>platform = \"wsl2\"</code>.\n2419|\n2420|### 6.3 Field constraints\n2421|\n2422|<code>platform</code> is one of <code>macos</code>, <code>linux</code>,\n2423|<code>wsl2</code>, or <code>windows</code>. A peer is authorized only when its\n2424|stable <code>host_id</code> appears in <code>mesh.peers</code> and the SSH\n2425|endpoint resolves to the authenticated host expected by SSH host-key policy.\n2426|Tailscale discovery MAY suggest a candidate configuration but MUST NOT write or\n2427|authorize it automatically.\n(more lines below)"}}}
